<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<TcPlcObject Version="1.1.0.1">
  <DUT Name="ST_VWC_NO" Id="{00904a76-0b8d-4527-b20a-255e2a186ec3}">
    <Declaration><![CDATA[(*
    ST_VWC_NO - status/interface struct of a normally-open (NO) VWC gate valve.

    PURPOSE
        Extends ST_VGC - the gate-valve status record that carries the
        differential-pressure and at-vacuum interlocks, the alarm outputs and
        the interlock device names - with the close side of a normally-open
        valve: the close permission xCLS_OK, the EPICS close switch
        pv_xCLS_SW and the EPICS force-close request pv_xOvrdCls.
        A normally-open valve rests OPEN when it is de-energized, so the side
        that needs a permission here is CLOSING, not opening. The open side
        (pv_xOPN_SW, xOPN_OK, pv_xOvrdOpn, xOverrideMode) is inherited from
        ST_ValveBase through ST_VGC and is not repeated in this type.
        All three members declared here are BOOL, so no engineering units
        apply. Each member states its TRUE state, and repeats the pytmc
        ZNAM/ONAM text, so the PLC value and the EPICS record cannot drift
        apart unnoticed.

    USAGE
        The pytmc-tagged valve record exposed by the function block that drives
        a normally-open VWC valve, in the same way ST_VRC_NO is the record of
        FB_VRC_NO / FB_VRC_DA and ST_VCC_NO is the record of
        FB_TurbVentvalve_NO. An integrator wiring the HMI or IOC reads xCLS_OK
        to know whether closing is permitted, writes pv_xCLS_SW to request a
        state, and may pulse pv_xOvrdCls to force the valve closed.
        Nothing in this repository references ST_VWC_NO today: it is a
        <Compile> item in L2SIVacuum.plcproj, so TwinCAT compiles it, but no
        POU declares a variable of this type and no code writes or reads these
        members yet. Treat it as the defined interface only; the close-side
        logic that ST_VRC_NO gets from FB_VRC_NO_FFO / FB_VRC_DA still has to
        be written for this type.
        When that block is written: pv_xCLS_SW is EPICS read/write, so it holds
        the last commanded state as well as the request, and it must be cleared
        when xCLS_OK drops, otherwise a stale close request survives the loss of
        the close permission (see the reset in FB_VRC_NO_FFO).

    OBJECTIVES
        Make the polarity of the close side explicit in the type definition, so
        that nobody has to read a function block to learn that TRUE means CLOSE
        and FALSE means OPEN on these PVs, and which member is a permission,
        which is a command and which is a force request.

    HISTORY
        2026-11-28 / Scirix Agent
            Added this header and an inline comment on each member (meaning,
            polarity, PV direction and units). Comment only: no declaration,
            data type, default value, member order or pytmc pragma changed
            (task #461, code-comment convention).
        2022-10-19 / jyin999 <jyin@slac.stanford.edu>
            FORCE_CLS / FORCE_OPN field names fixed (commit 17a55e1).
        2020-11-04 / ghalym <mghaly@slac.stanford.edu>
            Type added to the library together with the normally-open valve
            records it mirrors (commit 55b7003).
*)
TYPE ST_VWC_NO EXTENDS ST_VGC:
STRUCT
(* Readbacks *)
    // Normally-open variant: TRUE = CLOSE and FALSE = OPEN on the PVs below.
    (* Close permission (PV CLS_OK, EPICS input, io: i): TRUE = closing this
       normally-open valve is permitted; FALSE = closing is inhibited and the
       valve is held open, which is also its de-energized state. Set by the
       owning function block from the close interlock, not by the operator.
       Boolean, no units. The PV maps TRUE to ONAM "CLOSE" and FALSE to ZNAM
       "OPEN", so the record reads CLOSE (not "OK") while TRUE. Default TRUE. *)
    {attribute 'pytmc' := '
    pv: CLS_OK;
    field: ONAM CLOSE;
    field: ZNAM OPEN;
    io: i;
    '}
    xCLS_OK : BOOL := TRUE;
    (* Close switch, EPICS read/write (PV CLS_SW, io: io): TRUE = request the
       valve CLOSED, FALSE = request it OPEN. Because a normally-open valve
       fails open, FALSE is both the open request and the de-energized state.
       Must be cleared by the owning function block when the close permission
       xCLS_OK is FALSE. Boolean, no units. *)
    {attribute 'pytmc' := '
    pv: CLS_SW ;
    field: ONAM CLOSE;
    field: ZNAM OPEN;
    io: io ;
    '}
    pv_xCLS_SW	: BOOL;
    (* Force-close request, EPICS read/write (PV FORCE_CLS, io: io): TRUE =
       request the valve CLOSED regardless of pv_xCLS_SW, so a valve whose close
       side is not otherwise permitted can still be shut. Honoured only while
       the inherited xOverrideMode (PV OVRD_ON) is TRUE, and only after a
       qualification time: the sibling normally-open blocks qualify it with
       tOvrd (T#10s in this library) and clear the request once xCLS_OK has
       been TRUE for 10 s, so it is never a permanent bypass of the interlocks.
       Boolean, no units. *)
    {attribute 'pytmc' := '
    pv: FORCE_CLS;
    field: ONAM FORCE CLOSE;
    field: ZNAM FALSE;
    io: io;
    '}
    pv_xOvrdCls	:	BOOL;

END_STRUCT
END_TYPE]]></Declaration>
  </DUT>
</TcPlcObject>